  "history": {
    "origins": "Viersche (also Hundert or Hunderte) is a point-trick game local to the Wolf and Kinzig valleys in the Black Forest of Germany. It was first recorded on a 1932 map of the most common card games in Baden, republished in Friedrich Schlager's 1951 article on Cego; the centre of play was the Wolf valley, with other clusters near Oberharmersbach, Baiersbronn, St. Georgen, Villingen and Geisingen. It is the forward version of Hindersche (Swabian: viersche = forwards, hindersche = backwards), which was played by foresters, farmers and journeymen in the old Principality of Fürstenberg; anticlockwise play and the ranking of the ten suggest the game may be two or three centuries old. It is likely a descendant of Réunion, a German game once popular in the Rhineland and along the Neckar. Since at least 2003, annual tournaments have been held around Epiphany in Bad Rippoldsau.",
    "originCountries": ["Germany"],
    "timeline": ["20th century: documented in Black Forest region as a regional partnership trick-taking game."],
